1
00:00:00,046 --> 00:00:06,212
Over the past few months, we've seen multiple companies release AI teammates which can assist you with everyday tasks.

2
00:00:06,670 --> 00:00:10,212
The first one to do it was XAI with their Grok bot.

3
00:00:10,480 --> 00:00:14,080
Shortly after, Meta followed with its own version called Muse.

4
00:00:14,240 --> 00:00:17,440
And just a few days ago, OpenAI introduced dots.

5
00:00:17,760 --> 00:00:26,276
You might remember that there was a product before any of these companies released their own called OpenClaw, which was the first AI that actually did things.

6
00:00:26,480 --> 00:00:28,560
You could book a flight or send an email.

7
00:00:28,814 --> 00:00:37,440
So that begs the question: why are these companies gaining so much traction if something like that already exists and it's completely open source and free?

8
00:00:37,600 --> 00:00:42,080
Primarily, it is because OpenClaw was mostly intended for tech-savvy people.

9
00:00:42,160 --> 00:00:44,480
But it was also because it was ahead of its time.

10
00:00:44,640 --> 00:00:49,440
During its initial release, the models simply weren't as intelligent as they are today.

11
00:00:49,520 --> 00:00:53,412
And you could often see examples of OpenClaw failing at simple tasks.

12
00:00:53,680 --> 00:00:56,080
All of these companies saw that as an opportunity.

13
00:00:56,240 --> 00:01:06,400
They decided to rebuild their own OpenClaw, package it into a familiar user experience, give it official connectors to other companies, and power it by an intelligent model.

14
00:01:06,560 --> 00:01:14,276
Which is why in this video I'm gonna teach you how to build your own product like Grok bot, OpenAI Dots, or Metamuse.

15
00:01:14,640 --> 00:01:15,680
And this is the result.

16
00:01:15,840 --> 00:01:17,040
Let me show you what it can do.

17
00:01:17,134 --> 00:01:22,000
I'm gonna ask my agent to book me a flight from London to Barcelona on Friday morning.

18
00:01:22,160 --> 00:01:25,040
Make it nonstop and nothing before 7 a.m.

19
00:01:25,280 --> 00:01:30,240
The agent can use its desktop to open a browser and search for the flight I requested.

20
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:33,316
Once it finds the flight, it goes to the checkout page.

21
00:01:33,550 --> 00:01:39,332
In here, it enters the information it knows, but it gets stuck with information it doesn't know.

22
00:01:39,662 --> 00:01:44,560
In that case, I can simply take over the desktop and input that information myself.

23
00:01:44,720 --> 00:01:47,840
And just like that, me and my agent have booked a flight together.

24
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:54,160
Now I've purposely made this an example where a human has to take over, but your agent also has memory.

25
00:01:54,318 --> 00:01:56,960
So next time it can actually remember these details.

26
00:01:57,200 --> 00:02:02,916
I just told my agent that 10 new engineers start on Monday and they all need MacBook Pros.

27
00:02:03,310 --> 00:02:10,480
So ask distributors for the prices and get the best deal. The agent can use the email client just like you can.

28
00:02:10,702 --> 00:02:12,640
Simply give it access to your account.

29
00:02:12,880 --> 00:02:16,640
The agent can write the email itself and send it to the recipient.

30
00:02:16,800 --> 00:02:21,200
When it receives an email, the agent can open the email and reply itself.

31
00:02:21,360 --> 00:02:25,120
This way, you can give it a task to negotiate the best deal for you.

32
00:02:25,280 --> 00:02:30,320
Everything that you can do on your desktop, the agent can learn how to do on its own desktop.

33
00:02:30,480 --> 00:02:33,280
It's always gonna ask us for the final confirmation.

34
00:02:33,440 --> 00:02:38,960
In this example, I am confirming that I want to take this deal, and the agent will draft a reply for me.

35
00:02:39,120 --> 00:02:42,080
And just like that, the agent found the best deal.

36
00:02:42,240 --> 00:02:53,680
One of the most important features our agents can do is memory. I'm telling an agent in an individual chat to remember what is my staging URL, and it's written down in its memory file.

37
00:02:53,870 --> 00:03:00,960
So I can now go inside of a group chat with the same bot and I can ask it to pull out that memory from its file.

38
00:03:01,120 --> 00:03:05,600
This way, everything you tell to your agent stays as a part of their personality.

39
00:03:05,760 --> 00:03:11,012
The possibilities of what you can make your agents do in this project are truly endless.

40
00:03:11,360 --> 00:03:18,560
Not only that, but since we will be using agentic coding to develop this project, you will be able to style it however you want.

41
00:03:18,800 --> 00:03:27,680
I personally chose the Grok bot look and feel, but you can style it as open AI dots or meta muse, or maybe your custom design.

42
00:03:27,840 --> 00:03:34,000
Even though we will be using AI to develop this project, we are not going to vibe code our way through.

43
00:03:34,160 --> 00:03:37,600
We are going to know exactly what we are doing at every step of the way.

44
00:03:37,760 --> 00:03:44,520
We will be utilizing skills and MCPs to get exactly what we want deterministically without AI Slop.

45
00:03:44,880 --> 00:03:53,120
So without further ado, let's get started. Before we can start building this application, we need to understand how it works and the architecture behind it.

46
00:03:53,326 --> 00:03:57,040
The user interface behind this application is fairly simple.

47
00:03:57,294 --> 00:04:07,140
On the left side, we have a sidebar which is in a global layout, which means that the same sidebar appears for an empty root page,

48
00:04:07,440 --> 00:04:10,116
but also for an individual chat page.

49
00:04:10,382 --> 00:04:22,180
Besides the app sidebar, the only other components we have are the global command dialog, which helps us search through chats, and an individual desktop preview for each agent.

50
00:04:22,414 --> 00:04:28,960
The desktop preview is within a resizable panel, so the user can adjust exactly how much they want to see.

51
00:04:29,358 --> 00:04:37,936
And if they want to take over, all they have to do is click on the actual component, which then allows them to take over the bot's desktop.

52
00:04:38,096 --> 00:04:43,456
So what actually happens when I tell the agent to open the browser and type in hello world?

53
00:04:43,696 --> 00:04:46,576
How does it know to connect to a remote machine?

54
00:04:46,798 --> 00:04:48,804
How does it know how to open the browser?

55
00:04:48,976 --> 00:04:52,420
And how does it know how to type on that remote desktop?

56
00:04:52,718 --> 00:04:55,376
And why is it so simple for us to take over?

57
00:04:55,566 --> 00:05:01,056
When we submit a message from our chat interface, it first reaches the Next.js application.

58
00:05:01,216 --> 00:05:05,856
And in here, it's being processed by either a server action or a route handler.

59
00:05:06,016 --> 00:05:17,776
We could technically run the entire AI processing within that environment, but the reason we don't is because it wasn't built for long-running tasks like AI functionality.

60
00:05:18,016 --> 00:05:26,176
Because of that, our server action or route handler needs to pass that information to a stateless durable worker,

61
00:05:26,336 --> 00:05:29,572
which in our case is a trigger.dev agent.

62
00:05:29,856 --> 00:05:38,896
This agent lives outside of the Next.js environment where the rest of our application is running, which means that it has no request timeout,

63
00:05:39,136 --> 00:05:41,376
so long agent runs actually survive.

64
00:05:41,536 --> 00:05:46,016
Any crashes or deploys will resume the run automatically.

65
00:05:46,176 --> 00:05:52,260
Whereas if we had run this in our Next.js environment, it would unfortunately be interrupted.

66
00:05:52,496 --> 00:05:56,580
And it scales separately from the Next.js Web tier.

67
00:05:56,816 --> 00:06:00,676
And it is in this stateless worker where the magic actually happens.

68
00:06:01,134 --> 00:06:07,684
What we do here is we invoke a new AISDK agent and we give it tool access.

69
00:06:08,078 --> 00:06:15,428
Specifically, we give it sandbox execution tool, sandbox file tools, and sandbox screenshot tools.

70
00:06:15,856 --> 00:06:22,020
And with this combination, it is able to achieve 99% of the tasks that require computer use.

71
00:06:22,478 --> 00:06:28,096
So how are we able to see what's happening on the agent's screen and how are we able to take over?

72
00:06:28,302 --> 00:06:33,284
Well, the answer is in VNC, which stands for Virtual Network Computing.

73
00:06:33,456 --> 00:06:39,812
Every time we create a new agent, we also create an equivalent sandbox so it can access the computer.

74
00:06:40,174 --> 00:06:48,132
The sandbox has an open virtual network computing protocol using a no VNC web Sockify, which is open on a specific port.

75
00:06:48,814 --> 00:06:55,936
On the UI side, we start an iframe which loads a signed URL which can access that open port.

76
00:06:56,096 --> 00:07:03,108
And then once we click on that iframe, we load it in a full screen dialog and we enable the input.

77
00:07:03,376 --> 00:07:06,896
And from that point on, we can control the agent's browser.

78
00:07:07,056 --> 00:07:18,336
I purposely wanted to dive deeper into those two features, sending a message and controlling the agent's desktop, because I feel like they are the distinguishing features that make this project

79
00:07:18,496 --> 00:07:21,444
and what most of you are interested in how it works.

80
00:07:21,696 --> 00:07:30,976
Besides that, we're going to be using Clerk to protect our application and Neon in combination with Drizzle ORM to persist and store our data.

