01:15:30,144 --> 01:15:32,996
Use the simplest setup possible for an AI chat.

772
01:15:33,104 --> 01:15:40,704
And then tell your agent use your installed AI SDK provider. In my case, that is Neon AI SDK provider.

773
01:15:40,784 --> 01:15:46,384
In your case, it might be Anthropic or OpenAI, basically whichever provider you have installed.

774
01:15:46,544 --> 01:15:49,924
Your chat page should now have a composer at the bottom.

775
01:15:50,286 --> 01:15:53,060
So go ahead and send your agent a message.

776
01:15:53,264 --> 01:15:59,652
Very shortly, you should see streaming coming back token by token with the answer from your agent.

777
01:15:59,904 --> 01:16:02,944
The implementation itself should be quite simple.

778
01:16:03,104 --> 01:16:07,504
You should now have some new component rendered inside of your chat page.

779
01:16:07,744 --> 01:16:11,904
In my case, that is the chat component right beneath the chat header.

780
01:16:12,064 --> 01:16:21,252
The chat component itself should use the AI elements which we installed, which is used to compose the entire UI, render the prompt composer,

781
01:16:21,424 --> 01:16:25,184
etc. And the actual logic should happen within a route group.

782
01:16:25,344 --> 01:16:27,780
In my case, that is app API chat.

783
01:16:27,984 --> 01:16:35,344
And what's cool here is that you can find your provider instead of the stream text where the model is being chosen.

784
01:16:35,438 --> 01:16:42,144
So if you use anthropic or open AI, you can only choose between models from those providers.

785
01:16:42,304 --> 01:16:50,384
But because I chose Neon, I have access to all anthropic models, all Gemini models, GLM models, GPT models,

786
01:16:50,638 --> 01:16:51,504
Grok models.

787
01:16:51,856 --> 01:16:58,736
Kimi models, meta models, quan models, Lama models, basically all frontier models are available to me.

788
01:16:58,862 --> 01:17:02,336
And that's the benefit of using something like Neon AI Gateway.

789
01:17:02,496 --> 01:17:10,736
And best part of it all is that pricing is exactly the same as if I were to set up an API key with each of these providers directly.

790
01:17:10,896 --> 01:17:13,936
It's all just consolidated onto one platform, Neon.

791
01:17:14,176 --> 01:17:21,456
You can now tell your agent to use Railway Skill to add new environment variables and then stage all, commit, and push.

792
01:17:21,616 --> 01:17:28,132
We can now finally send messages to our agents, but we are met with another problem, and that is durability.

793
01:17:28,366 --> 01:17:37,380
Even though we explicitly told the agent don't implement any kind of persistence, let's say we told the agent implement persistence in a database.

794
01:17:37,776 --> 01:17:41,056
That still wouldn't exactly fix the durability problem.

795
01:17:41,296 --> 01:17:42,656
Let me give you an example.

796
01:17:42,990 --> 01:17:46,532
Tell your agent to write you a long story.

797
01:17:47,054 --> 01:17:52,548
Now imagine this streaming which is being returned to us is actually stored in the database.

798
01:17:52,750 --> 01:17:55,856
What happens if I refresh in the middle of that story?

799
01:17:56,046 --> 01:18:00,836
Do you think it would still be saved to the database or do you think the agent would be interrupted?

800
01:18:01,056 --> 01:18:03,536
The answer is the agent would be interrupted.

801
01:18:03,696 --> 01:18:06,336
This is why durability is so important.

802
01:18:06,576 --> 01:18:08,896
That brings us back to this diagram right here.

803
01:18:09,056 --> 01:18:11,204
What actually happens when we submit a message?

804
01:18:11,376 --> 01:18:18,336
The first thing that happens is chat UI submits a message into the NextJS application route handler.

805
01:18:18,496 --> 01:18:23,332
Now, route handler is great for a small demo or for very short responses.

806
01:18:23,616 --> 01:18:32,976
But the problem is it's by definition part of the NextJS application, which means if I refresh my tab, route handler breaks the connection.

807
01:18:33,056 --> 01:18:37,536
If I redeploy my NextJS application, route handler once again breaks the connection.

808
01:18:37,696 --> 01:18:47,536
If anything bad happens to this instance, the agent is interrupted, which is completely unacceptable for long-running tasks, which are the ones our agent will be doing.

809
01:18:47,726 --> 01:18:53,456
It's going to perform tool use and computer use and actually do things on the machine.

810
01:18:53,646 --> 01:18:58,256
That is why we need to implement this: a durable, stateless worker.

811
01:18:58,416 --> 01:19:06,816
We need to use the route handler only to invoke this task, which will run completely independently of our Next.js application.

812
01:19:06,976 --> 01:19:09,252
And in here we can invoke the agent.

813
01:19:09,456 --> 01:19:16,676
In here, we can implement tool calls, in here, we can call the sandbox and let the agent run as long as it wants.

814
01:19:16,896 --> 01:19:19,296
This is where trigger.dev comes in.

815
01:19:19,456 --> 01:19:27,936
We are now going to install dependencies and skills to teach our agent how to develop stateless machines where we can run our agents using trigger.dev

816
01:19:28,336 --> 01:19:29,188
AI chat.

817
01:19:29,376 --> 01:19:35,536
It's a good idea to create your account first so that you can easily connect it once you run the CLI.

818
01:19:35,696 --> 01:19:39,108
I'd also recommend creating a project from the dashboard.

819
01:19:39,310 --> 01:19:41,732
I'm going to call it AI Teammates.

820
01:19:41,966 --> 01:19:44,256
Now let's run the following command to get started.

821
01:19:44,416 --> 01:19:52,496
Make sure to select both MCP server and agent skills, which are important so we can teach our agent how to write trigger.dev

822
01:19:52,816 --> 01:19:53,056
code.

823
01:19:53,230 --> 01:19:55,268
Then make sure to select your coding agent.

824
01:19:55,456 --> 01:19:59,776
I'm going to select both Cloud Code and Agents.md directly.

825
01:19:59,886 --> 01:20:02,756
Now go ahead and choose each of these options.

826
01:20:03,022 --> 01:20:10,016
You will most likely have to do it twice if you selected multiple targets like I did Cloud Code and agents.md.

827
01:20:10,176 --> 01:20:15,524
If you choose to install the MCP server, simply select the coding agent you're going to use.

828
01:20:15,694 --> 01:20:19,536
I'm going to choose the project option for the scope of the MCP server.

829
01:20:19,630 --> 01:20:26,176
For the option of how you want to set up your project, I'd recommend the first one, which is scaffold it now with the CLI.

830
01:20:26,336 --> 01:20:32,548
This means you will have to log in, connect your account, create the trigger config, and an example task.

831
01:20:32,750 --> 01:20:36,516
This is pretty deterministic and both of us are going to be on the same page.

832
01:20:36,814 --> 01:20:37,936
So choose that option.

833
01:20:38,158 --> 01:20:43,460
Once you've authorized your login, go ahead and choose the new project you created, AI Teammates.

834
01:20:43,776 --> 01:20:48,416
For the folder path of the trigger.dev directory, I'd simply choose trigger.

835
01:20:48,576 --> 01:20:57,376
The default is source trigger, but because we are not using the source folder, it really doesn't make sense to create one just for trigger.

836
01:20:57,486 --> 01:21:00,416
So simply replace the placeholder with trigger.

837
01:21:00,576 --> 01:21:04,068
For an example task, pick simple hello world.

838
01:21:04,256 --> 01:21:10,016
Inside of your project, you should now have a new trigger folder with example.ds inside.

839
01:21:10,222 --> 01:21:18,096
In here, you should see a simple hello world task, which simply logs hello world, waits for five seconds, and then completes the task.

840
01:21:18,254 --> 01:21:19,856
Now go inside of your dashboard.

841
01:21:20,512 --> 01:21:24,832
Find API keys in the sidebar and click new API key.

842
01:21:24,992 --> 01:21:26,512
Give your new key a name.

843
01:21:26,766 --> 01:21:29,552
For simplicity sake, you can make it never expire.

844
01:21:29,742 --> 01:21:33,392
Give it full access and click create API key.

845
01:21:33,552 --> 01:21:39,792
Copy the value and then add it to your environment file under the key trigger secret key.

846
01:21:39,872 --> 01:21:46,432
You are now ready to start the trigger server, which runs independently of our next JS server.

847
01:21:46,592 --> 01:21:48,992
You can use the following command to get it started.

848
01:21:49,134 --> 01:21:59,062
Now that your trigger server is up and running, inside of your dashboard, you should see the exact task that you have defined within your trigger folder, example.ts.

849
01:21:59,438 --> 01:22:01,312
Go ahead and find the test button.

850
01:22:01,454 --> 01:22:03,392
If you want to, you can give it some payload.

851
01:22:03,552 --> 01:22:08,752
I'm going to use a very simple foo and bar and I'm going to click run test.

852
01:22:09,152 --> 01:22:13,632
What happens next is pretty uneventful, but that's exactly what we want.

853
01:22:13,792 --> 01:22:22,724
This example task is purposely defined to be as simple as possible to demonstrate that the agent can wait for five seconds and then finish the task.

854
01:22:22,926 --> 01:22:33,060
But what's actually important is that this entire code was never running on our Next.js server. In fact, I don't even have my NextJS started.

855
01:22:33,454 --> 01:22:34,980
That's what I wanted to demonstrate.

856
01:22:35,152 --> 01:22:44,832
This is the environment where we are going to run our agent code, a complete stateless machine separated from our Next.js environment, which isn't prone to crashes,

857
01:22:45,038 --> 01:22:47,712
redeploy problems, or network connection issues.

858
01:22:47,886 --> 01:22:52,912
Now go inside of your Cloud Code and verify that you can find all of these trigger skills.

859
01:22:53,102 --> 01:23:04,272
Now before we actually move our AI SDK from a route handler into trigger, let's stage and commit all of our changes so it's easier to keep track of what's actually modified.

860
01:23:04,398 --> 01:23:16,112
You can now tell your agent to migrate the existing AI SDK implementation inside of the route handler and our chat component, which is the composition of AI elements, to trigger.dev

861
01:23:16,432 --> 01:23:24,112
chat agent and add transcript storage for persistence and scope that persistence to chat ID.

862
01:23:24,272 --> 01:23:30,084
So yes, trigger also comes with built-in persistence and transcript storage.

863
01:23:30,286 --> 01:23:32,512
So we don't have to implement that ourselves.

864
01:23:32,782 --> 01:23:39,872
I'm also going to tell the agent do not add any new features, tools, or metadata except those needed for the migration.

865
01:23:40,032 --> 01:23:42,512
The implementation should stay simple.

866
01:23:42,702 --> 01:23:45,988
And then use trigger and AI SDK skills.

867
01:23:46,272 --> 01:23:50,432
You should now have a new task within your trigger folder called chat.

868
01:23:50,592 --> 01:23:58,672
Inside, you should find a very simple implementation of stream text which uses your AI SDK provider, but this time

869
01:23:58,832 --> 01:24:05,792
not within a route handler, but rather within trigger.dev chat agent, which is now run in a stateless machine.

870
01:24:05,966 --> 01:24:11,312
Now make sure you have your Next.js server running and make sure that you have your trigger server running.

871
01:24:11,472 --> 01:24:14,832
Now pick a random agent and send it a message.

872
01:24:14,992 --> 01:24:17,472
Wait for an answer and then refresh.

873
01:24:17,646 --> 01:24:28,272
This time both messages should be persisted, and the persistence itself should be scoped to individual chat ID, so different chats should have different storage.

874
01:24:28,432 --> 01:24:32,832
You can also test telling your agent to write you a long story.

875
01:24:33,006 --> 01:24:37,472
And the moment they start answering, go ahead and refresh the tab.

876
01:24:37,646 --> 01:24:45,152
Now it might seem that the agent has stopped responding, but let's see what's actually happening in the stateless machine.

877
01:24:45,312 --> 01:24:51,012
If you go inside of your runs, you will see that your agent is still executing.

878
01:24:51,342 --> 01:24:57,632
And in here, you can find the chat turn where I ask the agent, write me a long story.

879
01:24:57,792 --> 01:25:02,436
After a while, you will see that your agent did in fact finish the story.

880
01:25:02,752 --> 01:25:09,152
And that is what's important. That is why we implemented this entire feature to implement durability.

881
01:25:09,312 --> 01:25:12,432
Because imagine telling your agent to book you a flight.

882
01:25:13,006 --> 01:25:16,484
And then refreshing the tab in the middle of that task.

883
01:25:16,736 --> 01:25:22,336
No user would expect the agent to be interrupted just because they closed the application.

884
01:25:22,542 --> 01:25:30,884
So with this change, not only did we implement durability and persistence, but we actually went from an MVP to

885
01:25:31,056 --> 01:25:33,092
a production ready AI chat.

886
01:25:33,296 --> 01:25:38,496
Now take a look at the ridiculously small amount of code change we had to do.

887
01:25:38,656 --> 01:25:43,748
First things first, we got rid of the route handler because it's no longer needed for anything.

888
01:25:43,936 --> 01:25:52,896
We moved the entire stream text into the chat agent task directly using the same provider and model as we did in the route handler.

889
01:25:52,976 --> 01:25:59,536
You can now tell your agent to use the railway skill to sync environment variables, stage all, commit, and push.

890
01:25:59,696 --> 01:26:08,356
It appears that our builds are failing and have been failing since the last chapter when we added AI elements. I just never noticed this.

891
01:26:08,782 --> 01:26:16,816
Lucky for us, these are just type errors, so we can simply tell our agent to use the railway skill to debug and fix all of these issues.

892
01:26:17,102 --> 01:26:22,020
I'm going to tell my agent that deployment build is failing because type check fails.

893
01:26:22,336 --> 01:26:25,776
Resolve all issues and redeploy until it's successful.

894
01:26:25,936 --> 01:26:32,164
I can now verify that the deployment has been successful, meaning that the agent has resolved all of the type issues.

895
01:26:32,336 --> 01:26:36,016
Even though the deployment is successful, it looks like the page fails to load.

896
01:26:36,238 --> 01:26:37,216
So let's debug why.

897
01:26:37,376 --> 01:26:41,732
