01:29:47,086 --> 01:29:48,800
And for that, we're going to be using Daytona.

943
01:29:49,040 --> 01:29:51,040
First things first, let's install the package.

944
01:29:51,200 --> 01:29:55,040
Next, let's find the skill to teach our agent how to use Daytona.

945
01:29:55,280 --> 01:29:58,960
You can almost certainly find this in every tool's documentation.

946
01:29:59,278 --> 01:30:01,412
Simply find something like agent skills.

947
01:30:01,680 --> 01:30:05,120
And in here, you can find the exact command you need to run.

948
01:30:05,262 --> 01:30:09,360
Now go ahead and create an account so that you can create a new API key.

949
01:30:09,440 --> 01:30:15,120
By default, Daytona gives you $100 in credits, which is more than enough to complete this tutorial.

950
01:30:15,200 --> 01:30:22,820
But if you want to, you can redeem coupon code Antonio100, which will give you an additional $100 in Daytona credits.

951
01:30:23,040 --> 01:30:27,200
Now head into the API keys section and click create an API key.

952
01:30:27,360 --> 01:30:29,924
Give it a name and full access permissions.

953
01:30:30,160 --> 01:30:36,960
Now add both API key and API URL into their respective variables within your environment file.

954
01:30:37,134 --> 01:30:44,132
You can verify whether the names for the environment variables are still the same by looking inside of Daytona TypeScript documentation.

955
01:30:44,494 --> 01:30:52,320
In here, under configuration, I can find that the environment variables are Daytona API key and Daytona API URL.

956
01:30:52,480 --> 01:31:01,280
Or since we simply gave our agents skills, you can just ask the agent, what are the environment variables I need to add for Daytona TypeScriptSDK?

957
01:31:01,440 --> 01:31:09,680
Since we have quite a lot of changes by adding the skills, let's do a stage all and commit so that we can clearly see the changes we do going forward.

958
01:31:09,840 --> 01:31:13,092
Here's a fun thing that happened once I told my agent to commit.

959
01:31:13,390 --> 01:31:20,800
It actually changed my branch to feature Daytona SDK, even though in our agents.md we explicitly forbid that.

960
01:31:21,070 --> 01:31:26,400
So I'm gonna tell my agent that we explicitly forbid changing branches unless the user requests so.

961
01:31:26,510 --> 01:31:30,320
and force the rule even further and keep everything on the main branch.

962
01:31:30,480 --> 01:31:36,800
It isn't a big deal if the agent changes a branch, but I feel like it's just easier to have everything on the main branch.

963
01:31:36,960 --> 01:31:50,020
If you want to know the symptom of why this happened, it is because when we added the trigger in the last chapter, it created a Claude.md file. And in here it simply added use trigger.dev skills.

964
01:31:50,400 --> 01:32:00,516
And I simply assumed that the agent was going to continue to read both agents and Claude, but it looks like if only agents.md is present, then all agents will read that file.

965
01:32:00,720 --> 01:32:05,060
But if I have an explicit Claude.md, Claude is going to read this file.

966
01:32:05,518 --> 01:32:11,440
So it's a pretty good idea for you to add agents.md reference inside of your Claude MD file.

967
01:32:11,630 --> 01:32:15,012
This way your agent will have to read both files regardless.

968
01:32:15,200 --> 01:32:18,400
And that is why this mistake has actually happened.

969
01:32:18,638 --> 01:32:24,000
Since I just modified a couple of files, I'm once again going to tell my agent to stage all and commit.

970
01:32:24,160 --> 01:32:29,668
You can now tell your agent to use Daytona skill to create a sandbox for each bot that gets created.

971
01:32:30,224 --> 01:32:36,304
Use or add a sandbox ID property to the bot schema to maintain the relationship between the two.

972
01:32:36,464 --> 01:32:41,124
For now, do not invoke the sandbox, start it, don't perform anything on it.

973
01:32:41,358 --> 01:32:46,064
We just want to create the sandbox on bot creation and delete it on bot deletion.

974
01:32:46,224 --> 01:32:52,164
Remember that once we created the bots schema, we added an optional sandbox ID.

975
01:32:52,686 --> 01:32:57,664
In case you didn't add it, the agent will add it because of this paragraph right here.

976
01:32:57,824 --> 01:32:59,904
