What we added now is observability and error tracking within this environment right here, our Next.js application.

1364
02:08:26,384 --> 02:08:35,364
But the majority of business logic happens in a durable, stateless worker, which is entirely outside of this environment,

1365
02:08:35,584 --> 02:08:38,244
which we just added observability to.

1366
02:08:38,766 --> 02:08:44,224
So what we have to do next is find a way to enable Sentry inside of Trigger.

1367
02:08:44,398 --> 02:08:49,904
We can tell our agent to use Sentry skills to integrate Sentry with trigger.dev.

1368
02:08:50,304 --> 02:08:57,156
Once the integration has been set up, tell your agent to add an error inside of your trigger chat agent.

1369
02:08:57,358 --> 02:09:02,692
Now make sure to restart your trigger server and try sending any bot a message.

1370
02:09:02,894 --> 02:09:06,916
And you should now be able to see that error inside of your Sentry dashboard.

1371
02:09:07,214 --> 02:09:17,252
So we now officially have end-to-end observability from our Next.js application all the way to our stateless machine where the agent actually runs.

1372
02:09:17,518 --> 02:09:23,076
Now tell your agent to remove any demo or test errors that were added and stage all and commit.

1373
02:09:23,264 --> 02:09:27,460
We can now track errors across both environments our application has.

1374
02:09:27,822 --> 02:09:31,584
But one thing we don't have is end to end observability.

1375
02:09:31,744 --> 02:09:33,508
For that, we can use logs.

1376
02:09:33,806 --> 02:09:39,780
So let's tell our agent to use sentry skills to enable and add logs throughout this project.

1377
02:09:39,984 --> 02:09:44,544
This skill will find all the relevant places where a log needs to be added.

1378
02:09:44,846 --> 02:09:50,144
It's going to use the sentry logger and respectively choose warn, info, or error.

1379
02:09:50,478 --> 02:09:59,760
It's going to calculate how long something took, all the relevant information for the action, anything to give you a clearer picture of what's actually going on inside of your project.

1380
02:09:59,920 --> 02:10:04,720
The best way to try this out is to just use your app as you normally would.

1381
02:10:04,880 --> 02:10:14,880
Ask your agent some questions, give it a task, open the desktop, just try and do things for a couple of minutes, and then we'll go into the dashboard to see what was captured.

1382
02:10:14,990 --> 02:10:21,200
You can now go inside of explore logs, and in here, logs will start to load one by one.

1383
02:10:21,440 --> 02:10:29,200
Desktop opened, bot created, chat session started, chat turned finished, sandbox metered, group chat created.

1384
02:10:29,360 --> 02:10:32,800
Everything that's going on inside of your application is now being logged.

1385
02:10:32,880 --> 02:10:41,040
Now, individually, these logs don't mean much, even though you could still make some specific conclusions just by looking at the logs,

1386
02:10:41,200 --> 02:10:51,040
but combined with errors, they give you a much clearer picture of what was actually happened before the error occurred. This can be extremely helpful with hard to debug errors.

1387
02:10:51,200 --> 02:10:57,680
Now imagine having all of that information, all of that stack trace, all of those logs, and the power of AI.

1388
02:10:57,840 --> 02:11:00,800
At that point, no error is unsolvable.

1389
02:11:00,974 --> 02:11:05,520
And the more information you can give to your agent, the better it's going to resolve the error.

1390
02:11:05,680 --> 02:11:08,836
You can now tell your agent to stage all and commit.

1391
02:11:09,040 --> 02:11:19,364
Now you could go through these errors one by one and resolve them, but you could also configure something called SEER, which is Sentry's code review agent.

1392
02:11:19,600 --> 02:11:21,920
All you have to do is connect a repository.

1393
02:11:22,080 --> 02:11:32,452
You will have to install the Sentry app onto your GitHub organization or profile. If you choose individual repositories, make sure that you select the one where you want to add it.

1394
02:11:32,720 --> 02:11:38,240
Once you have your repository connected, you can go back into the configure Seer section.

1395
02:11:38,446 --> 02:11:42,240
And this time you will be asked to enable autofix on a project.

1396
02:11:42,400 --> 02:11:47,200
Make sure to select AI teammates and make sure to select the equivalent repository.

1397
02:11:47,280 --> 02:11:49,252
And then click Save Project.

1398
02:11:49,422 --> 02:11:53,760
Now go inside of your feed to find any recent errors that might have happened.

1399
02:11:53,840 --> 02:12:01,280
And in here you can find the autofix option and you can start analysis using Seer, which now has access to your source code.

1400
02:12:01,454 --> 02:12:07,812
The Seer agent is going to find the root cause, explain why it happened, and give you the reproduction steps.

1401
02:12:08,014 --> 02:12:11,040
It will also give you the evidence and the file attached.

1402
02:12:11,200 --> 02:12:13,680
You can then make a plan to fix it.

1403
02:12:13,840 --> 02:12:19,332
Once you're satisfied with the provided plan, you can press yes, write a code fix.

1404
02:12:19,520 --> 02:12:21,796
And then you can draft a pull request.

1405
02:12:22,000 --> 02:12:29,316
And in a few moments, you will be able to open the pull request Sear has drafted, and then you can merge it into your code base.

1406
02:12:29,600 --> 02:12:35,360
And just like that, you can use Sear to auto-fix all of the errors that appear in your application.

1407
02:12:35,598 --> 02:12:42,980
So the same observability platform that has very deep knowledge of our app can now also fix every error it finds.

1408
02:12:43,200 --> 02:12:51,920
And even though we thought our app doesn't have any errors, look at just how many it was able to find by just using the application.

1409
02:12:52,080 --> 02:12:57,120
We wouldn't have known that we have any of those if we didn't implement Sentry.

1410
02:12:57,440 --> 02:13:06,788
You can now tell your agent to use the railway skill to add all new variables and use trigger skill or MCP to add all new variables used by chat agent,

1411
02:13:07,040 --> 02:13:09,636
followed by push or sync the changes.

1412
02:13:09,870 --> 02:13:15,760
Now my agent did this automatically, but if yours didn't, make sure to do a trigger deploy.

1413
02:13:15,920 --> 02:13:22,000
Inside of your environment variables, trigger agent should have added the sentry out token.

1414
02:13:22,190 --> 02:13:25,600
And same variable should have been added to the railway deployment.

1415
02:13:25,760 --> 02:13:30,320
After a successful deployment, go ahead and play around with your product.

1416
02:13:30,480 --> 02:13:37,360
Very soon, inside of your sentry dashboard, you should see a new environment detected production.

1417
02:13:37,454 --> 02:13:42,480
You can now separately keep track of all errors that happened in development and production.

1418
02:13:42,640 --> 02:13:46,400
Congratulations, you now have a production ready application.

